> build · deepseek-v4.1-flash
Fixed — pip is working again.

## What was wrong

`/usr/local/bin/python3` (Python 3.13.1) had **no `pip` package installed at all**, but its metadata folder was left behind:

```
/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/
├── README.txt
└── pip-24.3.1.dist-info/   ← metadata said "pip is installed"
```

The `pip/` code directory was missing, so every invocation failed with:

```
ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'pip'
```

This is why `python3 -m ensurepip --upgrade` didn't help at first — it saw the leftover `pip-24.3.1.dist-info` and concluded pip was already installed.

## What I did

1. Moved the orphaned metadata to `/tmp/opencode/pip-repair-backup/` (kept as a backup, safe to delete).
2. Reinstalled pip from Python's own bundled wheel — no network needed:
   ```
   python3 -m ensurepip --upgrade --default-pip
   ```

## Verified

```
$ pip --version
pip 24.3.1 from /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip (python 3.13)
```

I also did a real end-to-end install test (`six` into a throwaway target dir), which succeeded, then removed it.

## One recommendation

Installing system-wide works now, but on Debian with a source-built Python it's easy to break like this again. For project work, prefer a virtual environment:

```bash
python3 -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate
pip install <package>
```

Note: `/usr/bin/python3` (Debian's 3.11) is a separate interpreter and has no pip — that's normal for Debian; it would come from apt's `python3-pip`. The `python3`/`pip` on your `PATH` resolve to `/usr/local/bin` 3.13, which is what I repaired.
